    <statement><p>Simplify the expression <m>\displaystyle{\ \frac{1}{x+5}- \frac{1}{x+6}}</m></p>
<p>The answer has the form of <m>\displaystyle{\ \frac{f(x)}{g(x)}\ }</m> where</p>
<p><m>f(x)</m> = <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0001"/> and</p>
<p><m>g(x)</m> = <fillin characters="15" name="AnSwEr0002"/></p>
</statement>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>1</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <answer>
      <p>
        <m>\left(x+5\right)\mathopen{}\left(x+6\right)</m>
      </p>
    </answer>
    <solution><p>Subtracting fractions is easy when they have a common denominator, so first get a common denominator, then subtract:    
<m>\displaystyle{\begin{aligned}
    \frac{1}{x+5}-\frac{1}{x+6} 
    \amp =\frac{x+6}{(x+5)(x+6)} 
     -\frac{x+5}{(x+5)(x+6)}\\
    \amp =\frac{(x+6)-(x+5)}{(x+5)(x+6)}\\
    \amp =\frac{1}{(x+5)(x+6)} 
    \end{aligned} }</m></p>
</solution>
